.SH NAME
anpu-verify - Re-check one finding with fresh probes


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBanpu verify --finding  [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Replay just the probes behind one stored finding with fresh
controls and print CONFIRMED, REJECTED, or INCONCLUSIVE.

.PP
Supported detectors today: backup-file exposure (re-fetch the exact URL),
headers posture (re-evaluate the checklist), NoSQL differentials
(re-run the probe set). Anything else reports INCONCLUSIVE with the
recorded evidence for manual review — never a fabricated verdict.

.PP
Examples:
  anpu verify --finding backup-file-exposed-123
  anpu verify --finding headers-posture --scan scan-1700000000-1 --json


.SH OPTIONS
\fB--finding\fP=""
	finding ID (or unique prefix) to re-check

.PP
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for verify

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	emit the verdict as JSON

.PP
\fB--scan\fP=""
	limit the lookup to this scan ID (default: search recent scans)


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--config\fP=""
	path to a YAML config file (default: anpu.yaml in the current directory, if present)

.PP
\fB--data-dir\fP=""
	data directory for the scan-history DB and caches (default: os.UserConfigDir()/anpu; override with the ANPU_HOME environment variable)

.PP
\fB--verbose\fP[=false]
	show per-stage finding and warning counts


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBanpu(1)\fP


